The "D" classes in a C++ program are categorized into seven groups. These groups are listed below, along with a brief description of each:

1. C++: This class is the most common and is used to specify a single "D" class.
2. C: This class is used to specify a single "C" class.
3. D: This class is used to specify a single "D" class.
4. E: This class is used to specify a single "E" class.
5. C++ (C++): This class is used to specify a single "C++" class.
6. C++ (C): This class is used to specify a single "C++" class.
7. D (D+1): This class is used to specify a single "D" class with an additional dependency.

A C++ program can specify a single "D" class or any combination of "C", "D", "E", or "$" classes in addition to a C++ class. The preferred form is to specify at least one "D" class and any number of "C", "D", "E", or "$" classes.

Here's an example of a C program that complies with the "D" class specification and uses C++ libraries:

```c
#include <ctime>

void myCfunction(int i, char str) {
    printf("%s\n", str);
}

int main() {
    int i, n, c;

    // Specify one or more of the following:
    // 1. An "D" class.
    // 2. At least one "C" and "D" class.
    // 3. At least one "C++" class.

    myCclass.cfunc.C.myCfunction(0);
    myCfunction(1);
    myCfunction(0);

    i = 0;
    n = 1;
    c = myCclass.myCfunction;

    // Specify one or more of the following:
    // 4. At least one "C" class.
    // 5. At least one "D" class.

    myCclass.myCfunction();
    myCfunction(0);

    void myCfunction() {
        printf("%s\n", c);
    }

    myCclass c;
    myCtype myCtype;
    myCclass myCclass;

    int main() {
        c = myCclass.myCfunction();
        printf("%s\n", c);

        #undef myCclass
        printf("%s\n", myCtype.myCtype().stdout);
        myCtype myCtype;

        // Specify one or more of the following:
        // 6. At least one "C" class.
        // 7. At least one "D" class.

        myCclass.myCfunction();
        myCtype myCtype;
        myCclass myCclass;

        c = myCclass.myCfunction();
        printf("%s\n", myCtype.myCtype().stdout);
        #undef myCtype
        printf("%s\n", myCclass.myCtype().nothrow.stdout);
        myCclass myCclass;
    }
```

This example is equivalent to the following program:

