Get ready to unleash your creativity with Arduino! In this tutorial, we'll dive into the world of basic coding and create a striking black and white striped image. You'll learn the ropes by following along with this code:

```cpp
const int pin = 11; // Define the pin as output
pinMode(pin, OUTPUT); // Initialize the image to black and white

void setup() {
  // Create the RGB image and set the colors
  int image[10][10]; // Replace with your desired image size
  for (int y = 0; y < 10; y++) {
    for (int x = 0; x < 10; x++) {
      // Create the stripes
      for (int l = 0; l < 10; l++) {
        int col = (img[y][x][l] << 24) | img[y][x][l];
        // Draw the stripes
        for (int row = 0; row < 10; row++) {
          for (int col = col + l; col < col + x; col++) {
            pinMode(col, OUTPUT);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

void draw() {
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    background(1);
    for (int l = 0; l < 10; l++) {
      // Draw each stripe on each line
    }
  }
}
```

Throughout this tutorial, we'll be using this code as a reference. Make sure to follow it carefully to achieve the desired outcome.

Now, let's get started with the setData command to send the color data. We'll use the Serial Monitor to send the following command:

`setData(color, OUTPUT)`

The setData command takes four parameters:

* The data to send
* The data type (in this case, an RGB-Byte)
* The length in bytes of the data
* The type of data that the data represents (in this case, an RGB-Byte)

Next, we'll use the send command to transmit the data.

